#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133484 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bca46ab2-488a-5596-b96b-559463731556" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Images of the Standard Basis Vectors Under a Given Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "4748324b-8edf-5fba-90e1-90683887d463" :question/math-academy-id "e-5436" :question/problem "The linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ has the standard matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhat are the images of the standard basis vectors $\\{\\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$?" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nThe images of the vectors in the standard basis $\\{\\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ are the first and second columns of $T$, respectively.\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(i) &= T\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(j) &= T\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the images of the vectors in the standard basis $\\{\\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ by computing the matrix products $T\\cdot\\mathbf{i}$ and $T\\cdot\\mathbf{j}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(i) &= T \\cdot i = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(j) &= T \\cdot j = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133485 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2845913-5c76-57f5-a4d3-139a6513225b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Standard Matrix of a Linear Transformation Given a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "0db64243-ffa4-595b-8555-5ee369450557" :question/math-academy-id "e-5437" :question/problem "In the diagram below, $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf j$ are the vectors of the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$, while $\\mathbf{i'}$ and $\\mathbf{j'}$ are their images under the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$.\nWhat is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf T$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2211/Source/Images/9745a47c18db297afd57b96e183c0583.png)" :question/worked-solution "Every linear transformation in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nFrom the diagram we see that\n\n- the image of $\\mathbf{i}$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and\n- the image of $\\mathbf{j}$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133486 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b057dd5e-f80e-5d3f-84ae-b6aebd5800ea" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Standard Matrix of a Linear Transformation Given the Image of the Standard Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "ae7884f7-abb9-56ba-b685-f0eab27ff795" :question/math-academy-id "e-5438" :question/problem "Given that $\\{\\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j}, \\mathbf k\\}$ is the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the images of $\\mathbf{i}$, $\\mathbf{j}$, and $\\mathbf{k}$ under the action of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ are\n$\\langle 3, 5, -1 \\rangle$, $\\langle 0, 4,3 \\rangle$, and $\\langle -1, 5, -2\\rangle$, respectively, what is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$?" :question/worked-solution "Every linear transformation in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ can be represented as a $3\\times 3$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $\\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j}$, and $\\mathbf k$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2845913-5c76-57f5-a4d3-139a6513225b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Standard Matrix of a Linear Transformation Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8e0ddc0-66d3-55d2-93ad-6e49404acd38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132579} {:answer/id #uuid "f822649d-1c26-5d6c-a453-4e281dba3695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132580} {:answer/id #uuid "13615556-fa13-507d-9a41-e2520e9e127d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132581} {:answer/id #uuid "254ccc77-345f-571d-b215-75ea26d48461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132582} {:answer/id #uuid "3001a438-a507-5630-93f7-a548cb31f718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13615556-fa13-507d-9a41-e2520e9e127d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132581} :answer-field/id #uuid "f66081e9-c5ef-564f-b3c4-27cf0e332a17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132578}] :question/id #uuid "e98d2b13-3a52-5439-8add-029a6bea2d0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-43894" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2211/Source/Images/q-43894.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $i$ and $j$ are the vectors of the standard basis of $R^{2}$, while $i^{′}$ and $j^{′}$ are their images under the linear transformation $T$.\nWhat is the standard matrix of $T$?"}]}
